Q76. What should come in the place of blank number [1]? 
In the following passage, some words have been deleted. Read the passage intently and choose the most appropriate 
option to fill in each blank: 
The discourse surrounding digital sovereignty in the twenty-first century reveals less a genuine redistribution of 
technological power and more a performance of procedural inclusion. As Silicon Valley platforms consolidate their 
dominance over global communication infrastructures, the governance frameworks they propose often embed 
assumptions of market-led innovation and technological [1] ______ that systematically marginalise alternative 
architectural paradigms. The global South''s data ecosystems, rather than being valued as autonomous epistemic 
formations, are routinely recast through the extractive vocabulary of ''capacity development,'' a phrase whose 
institutional [2] ______ exposes its debt to earlier colonial administrative logics. 
Embedded within these frameworks is the presumption that non-Western digital economies must be ''modernised'' 
before they may participate meaningfully in global data governance—reproducing a developmental asymmetry that 
conceals itself as technical assistance. Meanwhile, discourse around cybersecurity and information disorder is 
increasingly structured around vocabularies of urgency and catastrophe. This framing, paradoxically, enables a mode 
of discursive [3] ______, wherein structural reform of platform monopolies is deferred in favour of incremental and 
often counterproductive regulatory patches. 
Demands for data localisation, algorithmic accountability, and platform taxation are not neutralised but instead met 
with strategic [4] ______, whereby surface-level acknowledgment of legitimate grievances is deployed to forestall 
binding systemic obligations. Civil society coalitions are incorporated, not empowered. Subaltern voices are platformed 
only to be curated, not amplified. Thus, genuine digital equity requires not merely better representation within existing 
governance architectures, but a fundamental interrogation of those architectures themselves. Absent such a reckoning, 
international digital governance will remain a largely ceremonial [5] ______, rich in declarations and frameworks, 
hollow in transformative substance. 
(a) hegemony 
(b) preeminence', '/question-images/SSC_CGL_T_I_Similar_Paper_Held_p14_q73_0.png', 'genealogy', 'doctrine',
            'vestiges', 'chronicle 
Ans.(a) 
 
